---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import eqEightKick from "../../images/eq-eight-kick.png";
import eqEightSub from "../../images/eq-eight-sub.png";
import eqEightSnare from "../../images/eq-eight-snare.png";
import eqEightFilters from "../../images/eq-eight-filters.png";
import reverb from "../../images/reverb.png";
import Card from "../Card.astro";
---

<Card top>
	<p>audio effects</p>
	<Card>
		<p class="mb-2">"Drum Buss"</p>
		<p class="mb-2">
			Drum Buss is a one-stop drum processing unit in Ableton that adds
			punch, warmth, and character to percussive material. It combines
			several tools in one device: Drive and Crunch for saturation and
			grit, Boom for low-end weight with a tunable frequency and decay, a
			transient shaper for controlling the snap of hits, and a
			high-frequency damping control to tame harshness. Because all these
			controls are tuned specifically for drums, it gives you a fast and
			musical workflow without needing to stack multiple separate devices.
		</p>
		<p class="mb-2">
			Its primary use case is on a drum group or bus channel, where it
			helps glue all your drum elements together and makes the kit feel
			cohesive. On individual tracks it becomes more selective — it works
			well on kicks that need sub weight, snares that need more crack, and
			claps that need bite, but it adds little value on hi-hats and
			cymbals where it can introduce mud and harshness instead.
		</p>
		<p class="mb-2">
			On individual drum elements, Drum Buss works best in conjunction
			with EQ Eight rather than in place of it. The recommended signal
			chain is EQ Eight first, then Drum Buss — you shape and clean up the
			frequency content first, then let Drum Buss add character and
			dynamics to an already well-sculpted sound. This way the saturation
			and Boom are working on something focused rather than amplifying
			unwanted frequencies.
		</p>
		<p>
			The key to knowing whether you actually need Drum Buss on a given
			element is to listen in context with the full track after you've
			done your EQ work, rather than soloing the element in isolation. Ask
			yourself whether the kick hits hard enough, whether the snare cuts
			through, whether the clap slaps. If something still feels like it's
			missing punch, weight, or character after EQ, that's your cue to
			reach for Drum Buss. If it already sounds right, leave it alone —
			unnecessary processing is how mixes lose clarity.
		</p>
	</Card>
	<Card>
		<p class="mb-2">"Reverb"</p>
		<p class="mb-2">
			Reverb is essentially a simulation of physical space. When sound is
			produced in any environment, whether a small bathroom or a large
			concert hall, it bounces off the walls and surfaces and reaches your
			ears as a series of echoes that blend together into what we perceive
			as the "room." Ableton's Reverb effect recreates this phenomenon
			artificially, allowing you to place any sound inside a simulated
			space. For vocal samples specifically, reverb is one of the most
			important tools you have. A dry, unprocessed vocal can sound flat
			and disconnected from the rest of your mix, and a touch of reverb
			gives it a sense of air and space that makes it feel like it belongs
			in the track.
		</p>
		<p class="mb-2">
			In house music you generally want to be careful not to overdo it on
			vocals, a subtle short reverb keeps the vocal present and upfront
			while still sounding natural. For other elements, reverb works
			beautifully on things like synth pads to make them feel wide and
			atmospheric, on snares and claps to give them a sense of size and
			punch, and on percussive elements to add depth without pushing them
			too far back in the mix.
		</p>
		<Image src={reverb} alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs." />
		<Card>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Input filter - </span>This determines
				which frequencies of your signal get fed into the reverb before
				it does anything. The Lo Cut removes low frequencies from
				entering the reverb, which is something you almost always want
				to do because bass frequencies in a reverb tail quickly make a
				mix sound muddy and undefined. The Hi Cut removes high
				frequencies from entering the reverb, which can soften and warm
				the reverb's character. Think of it as a gate that shapes what
				the reverb is actually working with before it processes
				anything.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Early Reflections - </span>These are the
				very first echoes that occur when sound hits the nearest
				surfaces in a room. They arrive at your ears quickly and
				distinctly before the main reverb tail develops. The Amount
				controls how loud these initial reflections are, the Rate
				controls how fast they modulate if Spin is enabled, and Shape
				affects the overall character and density of those first
				reflections. In practical terms, early reflections contribute a
				lot to how you perceive the size and immediacy of a space. A
				room feels small when early reflections arrive quickly and
				closely together.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Diffusion Network - </span>This is the
				main body of the reverb, the long tail that follows the early
				reflections. The graph controls how different frequencies decay
				over time, so you can have high frequencies fade away faster
				than low ones or vice versa. Diffusion controls how smooth or
				grainy the tail sounds, higher diffusion means a smoother and
				more blended tail while lower diffusion can sound more textured
				and complex. Scale affects the overall size of the diffusion
				pattern.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Predelay - </span>This is the gap in
				milliseconds between the dry signal playing and the reverb tail
				beginning. Even a small predelay of a few milliseconds can do a
				lot for clarity, because it lets the original sound hit your
				ears cleanly before the reverb washes in behind it. This is
				particularly important on vocals and snares where you need the
				transient, the initial attack of the sound, to remain sharp and
				defined.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Size - </span>Controls how large the
				simulated space feels. A small size sounds like a tight room or
				chamber, a large size sounds like a cathedral or arena. This
				works hand in hand with Decay.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Decay - </span>This is how long the
				reverb tail rings out before it fades to silence, measured in
				seconds. Short decay times feel intimate and tight, long decay
				times feel expansive and atmospheric. For a house music bassline
				you want virtually no reverb or an extremely short decay. For
				pads and atmospheres a longer decay can sound beautiful.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Stereo - </span>Controls how wide the
				reverb is in the stereo field. Higher values spread the reverb
				further left and right, which can add a real sense of width and
				space to your mix.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Density - </span>Controls how many
				individual reflections make up the reverb tail. Higher density
				sounds smoother and more lush, lower density sounds more sparse
				and you can sometimes hear individual echoes within the tail.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Dry/Wet - </span>The balance between the
				original unprocessed signal and the reverb effect. When Reverb
				is placed directly on an instrument or vocal track, you
				typically want this fairly low, somewhere between 15 and 30%, so
				the source sound stays upfront. When used on a dedicated
				send/return track, which is the more common and flexible
				approach in mixing, you set it to 100% wet because the dry
				signal is already coming through its own channel.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				<span class="font-bold">Chorus - </span>Adds a subtle modulation
				to the reverb tail which gives it a slightly animated,
				shimmering quality. The Amount and Rate controls determine how
				intense and how fast that modulation is. Keeping both values low
				gives a gentle, natural movement. Pushing them higher can make
				the reverb feel more lush but also less realistic.
			</p>
		</Card>
	</Card>
	<Card>
		<p class="mb-2">"Compressor"</p>
		<p>
			A compressor is a tool that automatically turns down the volume of a
			sound when it gets too loud, and then lets it come back up again.
			Think of it like an invisible hand on a volume knob — when the sound
			hits a certain level it pulls the volume down, and when the sound
			drops below that level it lets the volume back up. This keeps your
			sounds controlled, tight, and sitting nicely in the mix without
			jumping out unexpectedly.
		</p>
		<Card>
			<p class="mb-2">Sidechain</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Sidechaining takes the compressor a step further. Instead of the
				compressor reacting to the sound it is sitting on, it reacts to
				a completely different sound. In house music the most common use
				is sidechaining the sub bass to the kick drum — this means every
				time the kick hits, the compressor on the sub automatically
				ducks the sub's volume down for a brief moment, then lets it
				come back up. This is what creates that classic pumping,
				breathing low end sound that is the backbone of house music.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				The reason sidechaining the kick and sub together is so
				important is because they both live in the same low frequency
				range. Without sidechaining they will constantly clash and fight
				each other, making the low end sound boomy, muddy, and
				indistinct. The sidechain essentially gives the kick the right
				of way every time it hits, so the kick always punches through
				clearly while the sub fills in the space in between.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				To set up the sidechain in Ableton, place a Compressor on your
				sub bass track. Click the arrow on the left side of the
				compressor to expand the sidechain section, then turn on
				External and select your kick track as the source. This tells
				the compressor to listen to the kick drum and react to it rather
				than reacting to the sub itself.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Threshold is the level at which the compressor kicks in — in the
				screenshot this was set to -25.2 dB. The lower the threshold the
				earlier the compressor starts reacting. For sidechain
				compression on a sub you want the compressor to react every time
				the kick hits, so set the threshold low enough that every kick
				hit triggers it. Watch the GR (Gain Reduction) meter and aim for
				around -6 to -8 dB of gain reduction — this means the sub is
				ducking by that amount every time the kick hits, which is the
				sweet spot for a tight house music low end.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				The SC filter controls what frequency the compressor listens to
				inside the sidechain signal before it reacts. Setting the SC
				filter Freq to 80 Hz tells the compressor to focus on the low
				frequency thud of the kick rather than its full signal including
				the click and high frequency content — this makes the sidechain
				more precise and musical. Leave the Res at 0.71 and SC Gain at
				0.00 dB as these do not need any adjustment.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Ratio controls how aggressively the compressor turns the volume
				down once it kicks in. A ratio of 4:1 as seen in the screenshot
				is a good starting point for sidechain compression — it means
				for every 4 dB the sub goes over the threshold, the compressor
				only lets 1 dB through, creating a firm and controlled duck
				without being too extreme.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Attack controls how fast the compressor reacts when the kick
				hits. At 1.00 ms as seen in the screenshot, the compressor grabs
				the sub almost instantly when the kick hits, which is exactly
				what you want — it lets the kick's initial punch come through
				clearly and immediately ducks the sub out of the way.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Release controls how fast the sub comes back up after the kick
				has passed. At 30 ms as seen in the screenshot this is set quite
				fast, meaning the sub bounces back quickly and naturally after
				each kick hit. This is what creates that rhythmic pumping feel —
				if the release is too slow the sub will stay ducked for too long
				and the low end will feel hollow and empty between kicks.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				The Golden Rule for sidechain compression is the same as EQ —
				always set it up with your full mix playing. The kick and sub
				need to be heard together so you can feel the relationship
				between them. When the sidechain is set correctly the low end
				should feel tight, punchy, and energetic — the kick hits hard
				and the sub breathes in and out around it in a way that drives
				the groove forward.
			</p>
			<Image src={eqEightSub} alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs." />
		</Card>
	</Card>
	<Card>
		<p class="mb-2">"EQ Eight"</p>
		<div class="flex flex-row">
			<div class="basis-9/10">
				<p>
					Each filter has a chooser that allows you to switch between
					eight responses. From top to bottom in the choosers, these
					are:
				</p>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside">
					<li>
						Low cut filters
						<ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4">
							<li class="italic">
								High pass filters allow high frequencies to
								pass, unaffected, while cutting low frequencies
								below the cutoff frequency. This is why they are
								<span class="font-bold"
									>sometimes referred to as low cut filters.</span
								>
							</li>
							<li>
								48 dB/octave Low cut (cuts frequencies below the
								specified frequency)
								<span
									class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
									>First</span
								>
							</li>
							<li>
								12 dB/octave Low cut (cuts frequencies below the
								specified frequency) <span
									class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
									>Second</span
								>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
					<li>
						Low shelf (boosts or cuts frequencies lower than the
						specified frequency) <span
							class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
							>Third</span
						>
						<ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4">
							<li>
								A low shelf will allow the user to boost or cut
								all frequencies below a cutoff frequency.
							</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
					<li>
						Peak (boosts or cuts over a range of frequencies) <span
							class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
							>Fourth</span
						>
						<ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4 italic">
							<li>
								Peak, also known as "Bell", filters can be
								additive or subtractive, allowing you to boost
								or cut a specified range of frequencies around a
								center frequency. Bells are the most flexible
								type of filter available as these filters will
								allow you to define the affected range more
								precisely than any other filter type. A
								parametric bell filter typically offers three
								parameters; frequency, gain and Q. Frequency -
								this control allows us to define the center
								frequency of the filter. Gain - this control
								allows us to choose the amplitude by which to
								increase or decrease the selected center
								frequency. Q - this control allows us to adjust
								the bandwidth of frequencies that are being
								affected. The inclusion of this feature is what
								makes a bell filter 'fully parametric'.
							</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
					<li>
						Notch (sharply cuts frequencies within a narrow range) <span
							class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
							>Fifth</span
						>
					</li>
					<li>
						High shelf (boosts or cuts frequencies higher than the
						specified frequency) <span
							class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
							>Sixth</span
						>
						<ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4">
							<li>
								A high shelf will allow the user to boost or cut
								all frequencies above a cutoff frequency.
							</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
					<li>
						High cut filters
						<ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4">
							<li class="italic">
								Low pass filters allow low frequencies to pass,
								unaffected, while cutting high frequencies above
								the cutoff frequency. This is why they are
								<span class="font-bold"
									>sometimes referred to as high cut filters.</span
								>
							</li>
							<li>
								12 dB/octave High cut (cuts frequencies above
								the specified frequency) <span
									class="font-semibold text-cyan-600"
									>(Highlighted in light blue)</span
								>
								<span
									class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
									>Seventh</span
								>
							</li>
							<li>
								48 dB/octave High cut (cuts frequencies above
								the specified frequency) <span
									class="text-indigo-700 underline decoration-pink-500 decoration-wavy"
									>Eighth</span
								>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<Image
				src={eqEightFilters}
				alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs."
			/>
		</div>
		<Card>
			<p class="mb-2">Kick</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Every kick drum sample is different, so before touching any
				knobs, turn on the spectrum analyzer in EQ Eight and play the
				kick so you can see where its energy is sitting — this gray
				moving shape tells you everything about where the kick lives in
				the frequency spectrum.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				EQ Eight has two filter types you will use for the kick. The
				first is the Low cut filter, which cuts everything below the
				frequency you set, letting everything above pass through. The
				second is the Peak filter, which boosts or cuts a specific
				frequency while leaving everything around it relatively
				untouched.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 1 — Remove the Rumble. Set the filter type to Low cut and
				place it at 30 Hz. This removes ultra low frequencies that you
				cannot even hear but silently eat up headroom and make your mix
				sound weak and cluttered. No Gain or Q adjustment needed here,
				just set the frequency and the filter does the rest.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 2 — Boost the Body and Thud. Set the filter type to Peak.
				Look at the spectrum analyzer and find where the biggest peak is
				— this is your kick's fundamental tone and where its power
				lives. For most house kicks this sits somewhere between 60–100
				Hz, so place Band 2 there and add a gentle boost of +2 to +3 dB
				with a Q of around 1.0. Think of this as turning up the punch of
				the kick, but keep it subtle as a little goes a long way here.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 3 — Cut the Mud. Set the filter type to Peak. This is the
				zone that makes kicks sound boxy, hollow, and cheap if there is
				too much of it. Listen carefully for a cardboard-like or tubby
				tone and place Band 3 somewhere in the 200–400 Hz range, cutting
				by -3 to -4 dB with a Q of around 1.5 to keep the cut focused
				and surgical. Think of this as removing the cheap hollow sound
				and replacing it with something tight and solid.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 4 — Add the Click and Attack. Set the filter type to Peak.
				Place Band 4 around 3–4 kHz and add a gentle boost of +2 to +3
				dB with a Q of around 1.0 to bring out the knock and attack of
				the kick. This is what makes your kick audible on small
				speakers, earbuds, and phones. If your kick already sounds
				punchy and cuts through naturally, go easy here or skip it
				entirely — always let your ears decide.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				The Golden Rule — always do all of this with your full mix
				playing, never in solo. The kick needs to work alongside your
				bass, sub, and every other element, and the only way to know if
				your EQ is working is to hear everything together at the same
				time.
			</p>
			<Image src={eqEightKick} alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs." />
		</Card>
		<Card>
			<p class="mb-2">Sub</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Just like the kick, every sub bass is different, so before
				touching any knobs, turn on the spectrum analyzer in EQ Eight
				and play the sub so you can see where its energy is sitting — a
				sub sine will show most of its energy concentrated very low,
				typically below 100 Hz, which is exactly where it should live.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				EQ Eight has three filter types you will use for the sub. The
				Low cut filter, the Peak filter, and the High cut filter, which
				cuts everything above the frequency you set.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 1 — Remove the Rumble. Set the filter type to Low cut and
				place it at 30 Hz. Just like the kick, this removes ultra low
				frequencies that you cannot hear but waste headroom and clutter
				your mix. No Gain or Q adjustment needed here, just set the
				frequency and the filter does the rest.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 2 — Boost the Fundamental. Set the filter type to Peak. A
				sub sine lives lower than a kick, so look at the spectrum
				analyzer and find the biggest peak — for most sub basses this
				sits around 80–100 Hz. Place Band 2 right at that peak and add a
				gentle boost of +2 to +3 dB with a Q of around 1.0 to reinforce
				the core weight and tone of the sub. Let the spectrum analyzer
				guide exactly where to place it rather than using a fixed number
				every time.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 3 — High cut filter. This is the most important band for
				the sub. Set the filter type to High cut and place it at around
				150–200 Hz. This cuts everything above that point so the sub
				stays locked in its own low frequency lane and does not compete
				with your bassline, pads, or any other elements that live higher
				up in the spectrum. No Gain or Q adjustment needed here, just
				set the frequency and the filter does the rest. Note that you
				only need 3 bands for the sub — do not activate any further
				bands as they will be redundant and unnecessary.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				The Golden Rule — always do all of this with your full mix
				playing, never in solo. The sub needs to work alongside your
				kick above everything else, and if you have a sidechain
				compressor set up from the kick to the sub, the EQ and the
				sidechain work together to keep the low end tight, punchy, and
				clean.
			</p>
			<Image src={eqEightSub} alt="A bird sitting on a nest of eggs." />
		</Card>
		<Card>
			<p class="mb-2">Snare</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Just like the kick, every snare sample is different, so before
				touching any knobs, turn on the spectrum analyzer in EQ Eight
				and play the snare so you can see where its energy is sitting —
				most snares will show a concentration of energy in the low mids
				with a long tail extending into the high end.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				EQ Eight has three filter types you will use for the snare. The
				Low cut filter, the Peak filter, and the High shelf filter.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 1 — Remove the Rumble. Set the filter type to Low cut and
				place it at 80 Hz. This removes sub frequencies that add no
				value to a snare and only clutter the low end of your mix. No
				Gain or Q adjustment needed here, just set the frequency and the
				filter does the rest.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 2 — Cut the Boxiness. Set the filter type to Peak and place
				it around 200 Hz, cutting by -3 dB with a Q of around 1.2. This
				is the zone responsible for the cardboard, hollow tone that
				makes snares sound cheap and amateur. Sweep slowly around this
				area while the track plays and you will hear it immediately —
				cut until the snare sounds tighter and more focused.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 3 — Cut the Nasal Honk. Set the filter type to Peak and
				place it around 900 Hz, cutting by -2 dB with a Q of around 1.0.
				This mid range zone can make a snare sound thin and nasal if
				there is too much of it. A gentle cut here cleans up the tone
				without taking away the body of the snare.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 4 — Add the Crack and Attack. Set the filter type to Peak
				and place it around 3 kHz, boosting by +2.5 dB with a Q of
				around 1.5. This is where the snap and punch of the snare lives.
				Boosting here makes the snare cut through the mix on any speaker
				or playback system, which is especially important in house where
				the kick and bassline dominate the low end.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 5 — Open up the Air. Set the filter type to Peak and place
				it around 8 kHz, boosting by +2 dB with a Q of around 0.8. This
				brings out the sizzle of the snare wires and adds presence and
				shimmer to the top end of the snare.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				Band 6 — High shelf lift. Set the filter type to High shelf and
				place it at 12 kHz, boosting by +1.5 dB with a Q of around 0.71.
				This adds a gentle, wide lift across the entire top end of the
				snare, giving it air and openness without targeting any specific
				frequency.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-2">
				The Golden Rule — always do all of this with your full mix
				playing, never in solo. The snare needs to sit on top of the
				kick and cut through the mix without clashing with the hi hats
				or any other elements that live in the mid and high frequencies.
			</p>
			<Image
				src={eqEightSnare}
				alt="EQ Eight settings for a house snare."
			/>
		</Card>
	</Card>
</Card>
